// dispatch:domain — a field-service dispatch API, as one component.
//
// Written to be a GOAL, not a reference: nothing here is implemented, and the
// three parts are meant to be written at the same time by three agents against
// `CONTRACT.md`.
//
// Why this one, when the repository already has `triage:domain` in the same
// shape. Triage's three parts each import a capability nothing else was going to
// call, so a part that reimplemented one failed alone, in its own gate. Here the
// SAME capability is imported by two parts — `schedule` picks the nearest engineer
// by `geo:resolve`'s distance, and `manifest` filters by radius with the same
// component's bounding box — so a part that hand-rolls haversine can pass its own
// gate with numbers that are internally consistent and disagree with its sibling's.
// That failure needs two parts to produce and one composition gate to see, which is
// the thing three independent halves are for.
//
// `geo:resolve/coords` is also, at the time of writing, exported by `geo` and
// imported by NOTHING in this tree — one of the sixteen the contracts test reports
// as unconsumed. So this goal is additionally the first real consumer of a
// capability that has only ever been checked against itself.
package dispatch:domain@0.1.0;

world dispatch-domain {
    export wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0;

    /// Service requests and what has happened to them.
    import records:store/store@0.1.0;
    /// Their ids.
    import id:generate/generator@0.1.0;

    /// Masking the caller's details out of the notes a dispatcher types —
    /// `pii-redact` satisfies it. A hand-written `@`-finder is how `requests`
    /// fails: it answers 201 on a well-behaved body and the gate reads the
    /// compiled component's imports.
    import pii:redact/redactor@0.1.0;

    /// The request lifecycle as a DEFINITION rather than a ladder of string
    /// comparisons — `fsm-workflow` satisfies it, and its `IllegalTransition`
    /// already carries the current state the contract's 409 has to report.
    import fsm:workflow/engine@0.1.0;

    /// Great-circle distance and bounding boxes — `geo` satisfies it. Imported by
    /// TWO parts on purpose (see the note above): `schedule` needs
    /// `distance-meters` to pick an engineer, `manifest` needs `bounding-box` and
    /// `contains` to answer `within_m`. Two hand-rolled haversines agree with
    /// nothing, including each other.
    import geo:resolve/coords@0.1.0;

    /// CSV with the quoting rules already right. One seeded request is titled
    /// `Boiler leaking, badly` and a field with a comma in it has to come back
    /// quoted or the row stops having five columns.
    import csv:codec/codec@0.1.0;
}
